    q: "When would you choose RDS, Aurora or DynamoDB?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "RDS is managed relational databases like PostgreSQL and MySQL, where AWS handles backups, patching and Multi-AZ failover. Aurora is AWS's own MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible engine with storage replicated six ways across three AZs, faster failover and up to 15 read replicas. DynamoDB is a serverless NoSQL key-value store with single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. I choose RDS or Aurora when I need joins and transactions across tables, and DynamoDB when I know my access patterns and need massive scale.",
    code: "# Multi-AZ PostgreSQL with the password kept in Secrets Manager\naws rds create-db-instance \\\n    --db-instance-identifier orders-db \\\n    --engine postgres --engine-version 16.4 \\\n    --db-instance-class db.t4g.medium \\\n    --allocated-storage 50 --storage-type gp3 --storage-encrypted \\\n    --master-username appadmin --manage-master-user-password \\\n    --multi-az --no-publicly-accessible \\\n    --db-subnet-group-name private-db-subnets \\\n    --backup-retention-period 7 \\\n    --query 'DBInstance.{Id:DBInstanceIdentifier,Status:DBInstanceStatus,Engine:Engine,MultiAZ:MultiAZ,Public:PubliclyAccessible}'",
