    q: "What is a Lambda cold start, and what are the main Lambda limits?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "A cold start happens when Lambda creates a new execution environment: it downloads my code and runs the init code outside the handler, which adds latency. Warm invocations reuse that environment, so I create SDK clients and DB connections outside the handler. Key limits are a 15-minute max timeout, 128 MB to 10 GB memory, 512 MB to 10 GB of /tmp, 6 MB synchronous payloads and 250 MB unzipped deployment size (10 GB as a container image). For latency-sensitive APIs I use provisioned concurrency or SnapStart.",
    code: "import time\n\n# Runs ONCE per execution environment = the cold start\nprint(\"INIT: creating clients, loading config\")\nSTART = time.time()\ninvocations = 0\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    global invocations\n    invocations += 1\n    kind = \"cold\" if invocations == 1 else \"warm\"\n    print(f\"invoke #{invocations} ({kind} start)\")\n    return {\"invocation\": invocations, \"start\": kind}",
