    q: "What is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "Vertical scaling (scale up) means a bigger machine, like going from t3.medium to m7i.xlarge; it's simple but has a ceiling and usually needs a restart. Horizontal scaling (scale out) means adding more machines behind a load balancer, which has no real ceiling and also improves availability. I design apps to be stateless so they can scale horizontally with an Auto Scaling group, and I mostly scale databases vertically or with read replicas.",
    code: "# Vertical: stop, change the instance type, start (downtime!)\naws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids i-0abc123def4567890\naws ec2 wait instance-stopped --instance-ids i-0abc123def4567890\naws ec2 modify-instance-attribute --instance-id i-0abc123def4567890 \\\n    --instance-type '{\"Value\": \"m7i.xlarge\"}'\naws ec2 start-instances --instance-ids i-0abc123def4567890\n\n# Horizontal: just ask the Auto Scaling group for more instances\naws autoscaling set-desired-capacity --auto-scaling-group-name web-asg \\\n    --desired-capacity 6",
