    q: "What is infrastructure as code? CloudFormation vs Terraform vs CDK?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "Infrastructure as code means my cloud resources are defined in files, reviewed in pull requests and deployed repeatably instead of clicked in the console. CloudFormation is AWS's native YAML/JSON tool with state managed by AWS. Terraform uses HCL, works across many clouds and providers, and I manage its state file, usually in S3 with locking. CDK lets me write infrastructure in TypeScript or Python and compiles to CloudFormation. I pick one per project and always run a plan or change set before applying.",
    code: "# template.yaml (CloudFormation)\nAWSTemplateFormatVersion: \"2010-09-09\"\nParameters:\n  Env:\n    Type: String\n    AllowedValues: [dev, prod]\nResources:\n  NotesBucket:\n    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket\n    Properties:\n      BucketName: !Sub \"notes-${Env}-${AWS::AccountId}\"\n      VersioningConfiguration:\n        Status: Enabled\nOutputs:\n  BucketName:\n    Value: !Ref NotesBucket\n\n# Deploy it:\n# aws cloudformation deploy --template-file template.yaml \\\n#     --stack-name notes-app-dev --parameter-overrides Env=dev",
