    q: "How do you deploy a React (Vite) app on AWS?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "I build the app to static files and host them in a private S3 bucket behind CloudFront with Origin Access Control, using an ACM certificate (which must be in us-east-1 for CloudFront) for HTTPS on my domain. For client-side routing I set CloudFront custom error responses so 403 and 404 return /index.html with status 200. Hashed assets get a one-year cache and index.html gets no-cache, so I only need to invalidate index.html on each deploy. The S3 website endpoint also works for quick demos, but it is HTTP only and needs a public bucket.",
    code: "# 1. Build (Vite writes to dist/)\nnpm run build\n\n# 2. Hashed assets: cache for a year\naws s3 sync dist/ s3://example-react-site --delete \\\n    --exclude index.html \\\n    --cache-control \"public,max-age=31536000,immutable\"\n\n# 3. index.html: always revalidate\naws s3 cp dist/index.html s3://example-react-site/index.html \\\n    --cache-control \"no-cache\"\n\n# 4. Tell CloudFront to fetch the new index.html\naws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id E1ABCDEF2GHIJK \\\n    --paths \"/index.html\" --query 'Invalidation.Status'",
    output: "vite v8.3.6 building for production...\n✓ 48 modules transformed.\ndist/index.html                   0.46 kB │ gzip:  0.30 kB\ndist/assets/index-C7mYl2Pq.css   12.10 kB │ gzip:  3.21 kB\ndist/assets/index-BX3k9aQ1.js   238.52 kB │ gzip: 74.90 kB\n✓ built in 1.84s\nupload: dist/assets/index-C7mYl2Pq.css to s3://example-react-site/assets/index-C7mYl2Pq.css\nupload: dist/assets/index-BX3k9aQ1.js to s3://example-react-site/assets/index-BX3k9aQ1.js\ndelete: s3://example-react-site/assets/index-Old9xYz1.js\nupload: dist/index.html to s3://example-react-site/index.html\n\"InProgress\"",
